<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext is permitted to exactly two peers: 127.0.0.1 and localhost.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    The first end-to-end run with the http backend failed with:

      WORKER_FAILED type=BACKEND_ERROR Cleartext HTTP traffic to 127.0.0.1 not permitted

    Since targetSdk 28, Android blocks cleartext HTTP by default, and the block applies to
    loopback too. INTERNET is necessary but not sufficient: the permission grants the socket,
    this policy decides whether the platform lets the request be written.

  WHY NOT android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"
    That flag would permit cleartext to EVERY host, which would quietly widen the containment
    that makes adding INTERNET defensible. This config is the narrow form: base-config denies
    cleartext everywhere, and only the two loopback names are exempted. The app therefore still
    cannot speak cleartext to a remote host even if HttpInferenceBackend were edited carelessly;
    it would have to be edited to use https, or this file would have to be edited too.

  NOTE (build lesson)
    XML comments may not contain a double hyphen. The first version of this file quoted the CLI
    flag name inside the comment and aapt rejected it at mergeDebugResources, so flag names are
    written plainly here.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
